1
00:00:01,929 --> 00:00:04,752
[SPEAKER_00]: This is in terms of self-mortification.

2
00:00:05,733 --> 00:00:11,580
[SPEAKER_01]: It's one of the extreme domains that the Buddha said one shouldn't go into.

3
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:15,124
[SPEAKER_01]: The other one is sensuality.

4
00:00:16,726 --> 00:00:21,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Usually, when a person hears that craving in terms of sensual activity is bad,

5
00:00:22,275 --> 00:00:31,004
[SPEAKER_01]: the mind or that person swings to the opposite extreme of self-torture, thinking that self-torture is now the... that's what I should do.

6
00:00:31,845 --> 00:00:35,189
[SPEAKER_01]: The belief that punishing the body will purify the mind.

7
00:00:36,590 --> 00:00:39,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, yeah, if I just punish myself,

8
00:00:40,857 --> 00:00:42,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, feel pain now.

9
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:48,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, everything that I've done bad in the past, all these afflictions, my suffering will end.

10
00:00:48,445 --> 00:00:57,955
[SPEAKER_01]: I will burn away all the past kind of consequences of consequences of my bad actions and end the suffering once and for all.

11
00:00:59,917 --> 00:01:03,661
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, the classic one would be sitting for long hours

12
00:01:05,259 --> 00:01:10,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying not to move, staring at something until, you know, it's painful, but you just endure it.

13
00:01:10,925 --> 00:01:17,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't eat, feel the hunger, you know, don't speak, don't see anything.

14
00:01:17,192 --> 00:01:19,695
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just shutting, trying to shut everything down.

15
00:01:22,317 --> 00:01:24,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So the Buddha says that this is not...

16
00:01:26,021 --> 00:01:27,343
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just actually read the definition.

17
00:01:28,863 --> 00:01:33,044
[SPEAKER_01]: These two domains should not be gone to by one who has gone forth.

18
00:01:33,804 --> 00:01:42,746
[SPEAKER_01]: The pursuit of happiness in sensual pleasures, the pursuit of self-mortification, both of which are ignoble and unbeneficial.

19
00:01:43,447 --> 00:01:53,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Without going to these domains, the Tathāgata has awakened to the middle way, which gives rise to peace, enlightenment, nibbāna.

20
00:01:58,425 --> 00:02:09,128
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this self-mortification, that's a... it is a common reaction to giving up sensuality, or trying to give up sensuality, sensual activity.

21
00:02:11,188 --> 00:02:14,949
[SPEAKER_01]: How do we know that we are actually not going into that domain?

22
00:02:15,949 --> 00:02:19,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And actually... because giving up sensual activity is painful.

23
00:02:21,711 --> 00:02:23,491
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that seems like self-mortification.

24
00:02:23,511 --> 00:02:25,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe I should just give into sensuality a little bit.

25
00:02:25,732 --> 00:02:27,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, I think that's the...

26
00:02:28,459 --> 00:02:35,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a certain amount of information and knowledge and learning needs to take place.

27
00:02:36,066 --> 00:02:43,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, I remember, I heard people say that before, that basically restraining, like being celibate, is a form of self-mortification.

28
00:02:44,633 --> 00:02:50,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have to endure your desires, therefore you're torturing yourself, therefore that's a wrong way.

29
00:02:51,479 --> 00:02:55,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, the alternative to that is then sensuality, for that person.

30
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:56,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

31
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:58,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Because... so they don't see anything in the middle.

32
00:02:59,001 --> 00:03:10,663
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you might feel like self-mortification initially, when you say no to your desires and take on training in the precepts, but you have to go through certain amount of withdrawal.

33
00:03:13,364 --> 00:03:21,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you were dependent on certain types of drugs and so on, not taking them anymore will hurt for a bit,

34
00:03:22,762 --> 00:03:23,142
[SPEAKER_00]: How long?

35
00:03:23,162 --> 00:03:25,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it depends how dependent you were.

36
00:03:26,364 --> 00:03:36,391
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you would... you would... well, you wouldn't... you would cease to suffer on account of withdrawal from those same things.

37
00:03:38,532 --> 00:03:43,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how to differentiate mortification versus sensual indulgence?

38
00:03:43,315 --> 00:03:50,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, take on the precepts and don't take on any other observances that have nothing... that are not mentioned in the precepts.

39
00:03:54,548 --> 00:04:21,493
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just what's going to happen is, a mind of doubt is going to start besetting you, because you take on the precepts, you develop sense of a container, and so on, but then, yeah, sensuality would come back, desires would be prompted by sight sounds, smell, taste, touches, by this or that, and that would be painful, obviously, because you blocked the only outlet that you knew, which was acting out of its sensuality,

40
00:04:23,017 --> 00:04:24,358
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't want to stay with that pain.

41
00:04:25,199 --> 00:04:26,000
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to get rid of it.

42
00:04:27,841 --> 00:04:46,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only ways of getting rid of it is either give back to into sensuality and engage with it again, or perhaps try and get rid of the whole thing, which is always practically manifested through, oh, let me introduce more things that I want to do, more observances.

43
00:04:47,467 --> 00:04:55,410
[SPEAKER_00]: More tightening, so that this state of mind that bothers me, affected with desire and doubt, so it doesn't come up at all.

44
00:04:57,010 --> 00:05:03,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how people fall into self-mortification and observances and being bound by Sīlabhāta Paramāsa.

45
00:05:04,373 --> 00:05:13,596
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because the pains of desires and hindrances that are revealed through training in the precepts and sense restraint, they don't want to bear them.

46
00:05:13,936 --> 00:05:14,817
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't want to stay with them.

47
00:05:14,837 --> 00:05:15,717
[SPEAKER_00]: They want to get rid of them.

48
00:05:18,008 --> 00:05:23,411
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, since the only way of getting rid of it before was giving in to them, now you stop doing that.

49
00:05:23,832 --> 00:05:30,056
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now the only way to get rid of it is, okay, let me do more of other things that would, you know, prevent this from arising in the first place.

50
00:05:33,138 --> 00:05:37,620
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you end up, like, adding observances, adding the duty.

51
00:05:39,261 --> 00:05:44,225
[SPEAKER_00]: All because you don't want to stay with the mind states that were revealed through keeping the precepts.

52
00:05:45,212 --> 00:05:47,293
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's really what training in the precepts mean.

53
00:05:48,094 --> 00:05:51,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Staying with the mind states that are revealed through keeping the precepts.

54
00:05:52,796 --> 00:05:54,257
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you start to train in them.

55
00:05:57,318 --> 00:06:00,480
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not meant to shelter you from those mind states.

56
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:03,061
[SPEAKER_00]: They're meant to simply not make it worse.

57
00:06:04,062 --> 00:06:10,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Not allow the mind to escape you towards sense objects in the way it used to when you started acting out and so on.

58
00:06:11,266 --> 00:06:11,486
[SPEAKER_00]: But...

59
00:06:13,971 --> 00:06:20,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Once the mind is contained, once the the pressures, the desires, the cravings are contained through the prism, they're going to feel unpleasant, of course.

60
00:06:20,193 --> 00:06:21,233
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the training now.

61
00:06:21,373 --> 00:06:28,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Staying with that, now trying to introduce more rules, more techniques, so that it doesn't come up at all.

62
00:06:29,303 --> 00:06:30,024
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no, no, no.

63
00:06:30,224 --> 00:06:31,505
[SPEAKER_00]: You want it to come up.

64
00:06:31,765 --> 00:06:33,606
[SPEAKER_00]: It will come up, whether you want it or not.

65
00:06:34,187 --> 00:06:35,548
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to run away from it.

66
00:06:35,948 --> 00:06:37,269
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to try to get rid of it.

67
00:06:37,289 --> 00:06:38,270
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to act out of it.

68
00:06:38,410 --> 00:06:43,334
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to learn how to become immovable in face of it, while it's there.

69
00:06:44,135 --> 00:06:46,337
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how, then, you will truly not be touched.

70
00:06:47,423 --> 00:06:53,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if a person is suffering, they either try to get rid of that suffering through sensual activity?

71
00:06:53,969 --> 00:06:54,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

72
00:06:54,549 --> 00:06:57,132
[SPEAKER_00]: You give in to sensuality to get rid of the pain of the desire.

73
00:06:57,953 --> 00:07:06,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if the desire were not unpleasant, you would have absolutely... it would be inconceivable to seek the sense objects and proliferate and engage with bodily pleasures.

74
00:07:06,861 --> 00:07:07,621
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be inconceivable.

75
00:07:07,681 --> 00:07:11,405
[SPEAKER_00]: But because desire is unpleasant, that's why you head in that direction as a default.

76
00:07:12,361 --> 00:07:12,721
[SPEAKER_01]: And then...

77
00:07:13,622 --> 00:07:14,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you stop doing that?

78
00:07:14,503 --> 00:07:15,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop doing that.

79
00:07:15,103 --> 00:07:20,788
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you still act out in another way, which is, kind of, try to... okay, it's self-torture.

80
00:07:21,088 --> 00:07:27,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, you have the extreme examples of, I'll just try and destroy the senses, so that then these mind states that I have to endure, I don't have to endure.

81
00:07:27,714 --> 00:07:28,754
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll crush it, kind of.

82
00:07:29,055 --> 00:07:29,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

83
00:07:29,555 --> 00:07:39,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you... if you... somebody doesn't go that... in that coarse manner, try to destroy the senses or starve them out or something, they would still then, oh, well, let me do more things

84
00:07:40,504 --> 00:07:46,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So that senses will not come across anything that can provoke the mind.

85
00:07:47,587 --> 00:07:50,108
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is just, as I said, kind of...

86
00:07:50,749 --> 00:07:53,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, you will have... you will be doing that to some extent.

87
00:07:53,230 --> 00:07:55,151
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to protect your senses.

88
00:07:55,851 --> 00:07:57,392
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the purpose of the sense restraint.

89
00:07:58,312 --> 00:08:00,133
[SPEAKER_00]: But how far you start taking that,

90
00:08:00,878 --> 00:08:05,482
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to determine whether you are now crossing into the domain of self-mortification, i.e.

91
00:08:06,023 --> 00:08:12,369
[SPEAKER_00]: just trying to manage the senses, so that the mind states don't come up, because you don't want to have to endure them and deal with them.

92
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:15,191
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the fundamental difference.

93
00:08:18,174 --> 00:08:26,402
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a person can choose to restrain their sight, what they look at, and so on.

94
00:08:27,313 --> 00:08:32,402
[SPEAKER_00]: But, inevitably, things will come within range, you'll see it, and the mind will be provoked.

95
00:08:33,043 --> 00:08:35,727
[SPEAKER_00]: The provoked mind is unpleasant, feels unpleasant.

96
00:08:35,948 --> 00:08:40,896
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to stay with that particular type of discomfort, the state of mind.

97
00:08:42,125 --> 00:08:49,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead, you know, well, like the sutta says, let me... those other... let me not see the sights like a blind man.

98
00:08:49,169 --> 00:08:51,150
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, let me pull my eyes out.

99
00:08:51,471 --> 00:08:55,513
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, you'll stop seeing the sights, but the mind remained equally untrained.

100
00:08:55,933 --> 00:09:01,737
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, you even lost one of the means of accessing that untrained mind, revealing that untrained mind.

101
00:09:03,398 --> 00:09:10,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's important that you have to recognize... what you have to recognize is that you... it's important to not act out of the intention towards sights.

102
00:09:11,492 --> 00:09:18,335
[SPEAKER_00]: But at the same time, not to try and destroy the eyes, because you want the non-intentional sights to provoke your mind.

103
00:09:19,055 --> 00:09:20,276
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the training will be.

104
00:09:21,276 --> 00:09:27,939
[SPEAKER_00]: When you have no intention to look, smell, taste, touch towards any sensual objects, yet they come in the range, and yet the wild animal is provoked.

105
00:09:29,260 --> 00:09:30,800
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, oh, that's what...

106
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:31,701
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't do this.

107
00:09:32,381 --> 00:09:34,982
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the nature of the senses and the untrained mind.

108
00:09:36,822 --> 00:09:42,647
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the difference that training in the virtue will reveal, how your intention matters.

109
00:09:43,328 --> 00:09:52,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether the provocation came on account of you intentionally provoking it, intentionally looking, intentionally tasting, touching, smelling and so on, or unintentionally.

110
00:09:53,877 --> 00:10:03,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, you have to completely abandon the intentional provocation, intentional adding of the fuel, so that then you can discern the unintentional, eh...

111
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:10,142
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of the unintentional environment, so to speak, that then your mind reacts to.

112
00:10:10,222 --> 00:10:13,644
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that's where the training, that's where the communication can occur.

113
00:10:14,625 --> 00:10:18,727
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but again, if you don't know that, you would then think that's a problem as well.

114
00:10:19,387 --> 00:10:22,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it hurts, because it's unpleasant, because desire, pressure is still there.

115
00:10:24,290 --> 00:10:26,651
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's... and because of that, you want to get rid of that as well.

116
00:10:26,851 --> 00:10:33,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me then go on top of a mountain, pull my eyes out, block my ears, never see, smell, taste, touch, and I'll be safe.

117
00:10:33,355 --> 00:10:33,935
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

118
00:10:34,548 --> 00:10:41,511
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just gonna destroy the means of revealing the untrained mind, the five senses, and yet the mind will remain equally trained.

119
00:10:43,352 --> 00:10:50,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Or let me focus hard on some concentration technique where I'll stop perceiving any senses, anything that comes, and that's the peace.

120
00:10:50,635 --> 00:10:51,916
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's just shutting down.

121
00:10:52,636 --> 00:11:03,741
[SPEAKER_00]: The peace is when no amount of sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, unintentional or intentional for that matter, can provoke the mind towards desire, ill-will or distraction.

122
00:11:05,233 --> 00:11:18,842
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in order to get to that point, first you have to stop doing all the intentional acts towards those unwholesome things, in order to reveal the unintentional provocations on account of the wild mind, in order to calm it down.

123
00:11:19,283 --> 00:11:24,586
[SPEAKER_00]: When the mind is fully calmed, trained, properly understood, then intentional or unintentional, it's all pure anyway.

124
00:11:24,646 --> 00:11:25,627
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all purified anyway.

125
00:11:29,823 --> 00:11:31,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's self-mortification.

126
00:11:31,748 --> 00:11:33,172
[SPEAKER_01]: It's aversion, basically.

127
00:11:33,673 --> 00:11:35,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

128
00:11:36,139 --> 00:11:37,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Towards what you're experiencing.

129
00:11:39,105 --> 00:11:50,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is a sutta where the Buddha says that, you know, as a bodhisattva, as he was beginning to train, he thought, pleasure is not to be reached through pleasure.

130
00:11:50,850 --> 00:11:57,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, freedom from dukkha is not to be reached through pleasure.

131
00:11:58,314 --> 00:11:59,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure is to be reached through pain.

132
00:12:00,775 --> 00:12:01,415
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what he thought.

133
00:12:01,435 --> 00:12:03,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if I just...

134
00:12:03,376 --> 00:12:04,777
[SPEAKER_00]: If I just inflict the pain, yeah.

135
00:12:05,297 --> 00:12:07,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, eventually, it will get to this nibbāna.

136
00:12:08,118 --> 00:12:12,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but it depends what type of pain we're talking about.

137
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:13,041
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

138
00:12:14,381 --> 00:12:27,908
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have a pain of just bearing the elements, bearing the discomfort, bearing the power of your determination, sticking to it, and that will not necessarily result in anything, anything of value.

139
00:12:28,768 --> 00:12:33,671
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have, as I said, a particular type of pain that's not terribly unpleasant.

140
00:12:36,281 --> 00:12:37,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Particular type of pain.

141
00:12:37,241 --> 00:12:38,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Particular type of pain.

142
00:12:38,302 --> 00:12:45,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Pain of the recognition of the untrained mind from within, that you are containing, but not trying to kill or suffocate either.

143
00:12:48,744 --> 00:13:00,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like the pain of that mind, of that pressure, and then another pain that comes on top of it is the recognition of, oh, this needs to be tamed.

144
00:13:01,347 --> 00:13:02,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Where do I even begin?

145
00:13:04,167 --> 00:13:21,994
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, the doubt, now the pain of doubt, the pain of not knowing whether it works, whether not, where does sensory strain lead, and so... All of that, it's a lot harder to stick to, in terms of training and practice, than, oh, just tell me a technique that I will mindlessly adhere to.

146
00:13:22,294 --> 00:13:24,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, I won't have to think about anything else.

147
00:13:25,336 --> 00:13:28,217
[SPEAKER_00]: That there's a reason these techniques are popular and are mainstream.

148
00:13:29,038 --> 00:13:42,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, they feed into that very thing of, okay, so if I just adhere to this mechanical precepts, repetition, doing this or that, I don't have to feel the the life of this wild animal.

149
00:13:43,183 --> 00:13:50,465
[SPEAKER_00]: I can just keep, as it comes up, I could just keep putting new things on top of it, more and more, and with a hope that eventually it will stop coming up.

150
00:13:50,485 --> 00:13:50,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

151
00:13:51,446 --> 00:13:54,867
[SPEAKER_01]: So the measure, the longer I do it, the harder I do it, it's going to work.

152
00:13:54,887 --> 00:13:57,488
[SPEAKER_00]: The stiffer it is, the more rigid it is.

153
00:13:57,708 --> 00:13:58,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

154
00:13:59,417 --> 00:14:07,823
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, so it means that pain, purification comes through... Well, patient endurance, that khanti.

155
00:14:07,943 --> 00:14:09,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Patient endurance of what?

156
00:14:10,105 --> 00:14:13,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Not of anything that you think of or feel dutiful towards.

157
00:14:13,848 --> 00:14:21,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Patient endurance of the states of the mind that come up as a result of you imposing virtue and sense restraint as a form of training.

158
00:14:24,887 --> 00:14:33,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when... See, you could say there is a desire to look or taste something, and then you said no to yourself, because it was against the precepts.

159
00:14:35,092 --> 00:14:40,414
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, maybe it wasn't against the precepts, but it was clearly rooted in sensuality.

160
00:14:41,034 --> 00:14:47,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Say you wanted to eat something that was unnecessary, and you wanted to increase... you want to start practicing sense restraint, and you said no to it.

161
00:14:48,638 --> 00:14:48,958
[SPEAKER_00]: And now,

162
00:14:49,977 --> 00:14:53,499
[SPEAKER_00]: That desire, the mind of that desire, it's gonna rebel.

163
00:14:53,679 --> 00:14:55,420
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna... it's gonna complain about it.

164
00:14:55,460 --> 00:14:56,500
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna try and attack you.

165
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:59,142
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna try and get you back under its will.

166
00:15:00,803 --> 00:15:01,843
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's not pleasant.

167
00:15:01,903 --> 00:15:03,104
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just from inside.

168
00:15:04,084 --> 00:15:06,886
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like you're being... being basically threatened from inside.

169
00:15:09,647 --> 00:15:11,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the right training means staying with that.

170
00:15:12,049 --> 00:15:14,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Not acting out of it, but not trying to get rid of it.

171
00:15:14,882 --> 00:15:17,523
[SPEAKER_00]: However, as I said, it's unpleasant.

172
00:15:17,603 --> 00:15:19,404
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unpleasant in a very specific kind.

173
00:15:20,304 --> 00:15:23,145
[SPEAKER_00]: A specific kind that nobody will accidentally choose to adopt.

174
00:15:24,406 --> 00:15:31,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody will accidentally choose to stay with that particular level of discomfort that your own mind threatens you and so on with.

175
00:15:32,469 --> 00:15:39,972
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead, it's a lot easier than, say, you said no to this desire, now there's a... the mind of desire comes up, or let me now

176
00:15:41,122 --> 00:15:53,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Focus on my breathing, or do this technique, or watch my sensations, or, in other words, let me distract myself with anything else now, all the other things that are on paper said as wholesome, so that this mind goes away.

177
00:15:56,991 --> 00:16:01,913
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it might work, and it might work once, twice, it might work ten times, but you always have to do it.

178
00:16:02,934 --> 00:16:06,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever the mind comes up, you always have to calm it down, because you don't know how to deal with it.

179
00:16:07,757 --> 00:16:09,037
[SPEAKER_00]: And the problem is not that

180
00:16:11,520 --> 00:16:12,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I want to calm the mind.

181
00:16:12,941 --> 00:16:20,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem is that you're basically believing that you need to act and... and... and suppress.

182
00:16:21,166 --> 00:16:30,572
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind states that are revealed on account of restraint and virtue carries the implicit view that will then prevent you from uprooting it.

183
00:16:31,352 --> 00:16:40,018
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, prevent you from actually addressing the problem, so that the mind will... mind in itself will stop having the desire to pressure you with.

184
00:16:42,851 --> 00:16:49,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if there is... if the mind itself has lost the desire towards things you said no to, that's the true calming.

185
00:16:49,914 --> 00:16:51,295
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the true non-disturbance.

186
00:16:52,075 --> 00:16:54,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the animal is not reactive anymore.

187
00:16:54,116 --> 00:16:55,237
[SPEAKER_00]: It does not attack anymore.

188
00:16:55,297 --> 00:17:02,460
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to practice keeping the leash and restraining it and suffocating it and holding it down.

189
00:17:03,435 --> 00:17:05,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the virtue did that for you, basically.

190
00:17:05,276 --> 00:17:10,239
[SPEAKER_00]: It revealed the state of how deeply the animal was untrained, how wild it was.

191
00:17:11,059 --> 00:17:14,761
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, do you do more things so you don't have to do that work of training it?

192
00:17:15,821 --> 00:17:21,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you recognize that the real training is, okay, the mind of desire is present, okay, the wild animal is here,

193
00:17:23,845 --> 00:17:29,066
[SPEAKER_00]: If I try to run away from it, act out of it, none of that will result in communicating the right things to it.

194
00:17:29,607 --> 00:17:34,388
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first and foremost, I have to acknowledge that that's the state of mind for what it is and stay with it.

195
00:17:34,808 --> 00:17:35,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Know it as such.

196
00:17:36,528 --> 00:17:43,950
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why in that other sutta, when somebody asked the Buddha, you know, Master Gautama said that dhamma is directly visible here and now.

197
00:17:44,050 --> 00:17:45,190
[SPEAKER_00]: How should that be understood?

198
00:17:46,881 --> 00:17:55,563
[SPEAKER_00]: And people would, you know, often think how directly visible the here and now, the present moment, awareness, all sorts of mystical ideas about it.

199
00:17:55,723 --> 00:17:59,885
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha didn't answer any of that in that manner.

200
00:17:59,965 --> 00:18:08,667
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well, when you know the mind of lust as mind of lust, a mind of aversion as mind of aversion, that's how dhamma is directly visible.

201
00:18:09,808 --> 00:18:20,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because, you know, I can contemplate the Four Noble Truths, and the Eightfold Path, and the Paticca Upāda, or, as I say, go in the mystical direction and just start describing it in some, who knows, idiosyncratic terms.

202
00:18:20,871 --> 00:18:21,191
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

203
00:18:21,651 --> 00:18:31,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognizing the mind for what it is, as it is such, staying with it, is how you practice the dhamma, how you get to see the dhamma directly visible here and now.

204
00:18:31,194 --> 00:18:32,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Four Noble Truths, see the Four Noble Truths.

205
00:18:32,915 --> 00:18:34,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, everything else follows from it.

206
00:18:35,562 --> 00:18:46,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the recognition of the mind states that come as a result of the restraint, virtue, and so on, that's how you get to practice the dhamma.

207
00:18:48,291 --> 00:18:49,312
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you get to see it.

208
00:18:50,273 --> 00:18:54,276
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you get to see the principle behind that whole training that we described.

209
00:18:56,194 --> 00:19:10,126
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the self-mortification would always, as I said, would always kind of... it could have these, obviously, coarse extremes, but it actually goes as far as you have a mind state that has a reason, you don't want it, you're aversive towards it.

210
00:19:10,346 --> 00:19:13,729
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to get rid of this mind because it hurts me and that's why I hate it.

211
00:19:13,769 --> 00:19:14,449
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the beginning.

212
00:19:14,709 --> 00:19:16,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're acting out of aversion now.

213
00:19:16,851 --> 00:19:19,192
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you want to act out of essential desire.

214
00:19:19,232 --> 00:19:20,053
[SPEAKER_00]: You said no to that.

215
00:19:20,133 --> 00:19:21,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, there is a painful mind.

216
00:19:21,413 --> 00:19:22,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you want to get rid of that.

217
00:19:22,874 --> 00:19:25,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, oh, so now you're going to the aversion direction.

218
00:19:25,675 --> 00:19:27,116
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just wanted to get rid of that.

219
00:19:27,576 --> 00:19:36,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Adopting sets of behaviors, rules, duties, techniques to get rid of it, to keep the lid on it, to keep it down, so you don't have to deal with it.

220
00:19:36,161 --> 00:19:37,801
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already a form of self-mortification.

221
00:19:37,821 --> 00:19:40,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you believe that by adhering to a certain

222
00:19:41,764 --> 00:19:46,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Technique, or just mindless adherence to the duty, or what Ajahn says, and I don't have to think about it.

223
00:19:46,170 --> 00:19:46,651
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just do it.

224
00:19:46,671 --> 00:19:47,171
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just do it.

225
00:19:47,191 --> 00:19:47,672
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just do it.

226
00:19:48,213 --> 00:19:51,557
[SPEAKER_00]: That will free my mind from these wild tendencies.

227
00:19:51,637 --> 00:19:52,298
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

228
00:19:53,059 --> 00:19:55,202
[SPEAKER_00]: You can... and you can keep the lid forcefully.

229
00:19:56,100 --> 00:20:06,749
[SPEAKER_00]: For decades, if you want, the day will come when you forget about the lid, when you won't close it properly, for one reason or the other, and the same wild animal is going to come out.

230
00:20:07,590 --> 00:20:10,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And because nothing has changed in terms of its internal tendencies.

231
00:20:11,393 --> 00:20:14,436
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the deer herd sutta that we mentioned so many times.

232
00:20:15,357 --> 00:20:17,179
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no, they shun the bait.

233
00:20:17,499 --> 00:20:21,783
[SPEAKER_00]: They shun the pressure of the bait, the proximity of the bait, and run into the mountains.

234
00:20:22,343 --> 00:20:27,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, they're safe there, and then they run out of food there, and they come back, because they have to eat sooner or later.

235
00:20:27,448 --> 00:20:37,757
[SPEAKER_00]: They come back, and they still didn't learn how to differentiate the bait, and not act towards the bait, and they get, again, hooked, caught, and then Māra does with them what he wants.

236
00:20:37,958 --> 00:20:38,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

237
00:20:40,127 --> 00:20:50,493
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that you have to keep a lid on things means, and you're running away, that that whole state means that you're running away from something, there's something you're keeping a lid on that hasn't changed at all.

238
00:20:50,513 --> 00:20:50,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

239
00:20:50,813 --> 00:20:57,977
[SPEAKER_00]: If you keep the lid on things because you recognize, well, I need to keep the lid on things for now, but you don't confuse that for the actual

240
00:20:58,918 --> 00:21:00,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Training of that which is underneath the lid.

241
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:02,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm trying to cover it.

242
00:21:02,241 --> 00:21:05,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you will be keeping the lid on things one way or the other.

243
00:21:05,243 --> 00:21:16,412
[SPEAKER_00]: But what I'm talking about here, it's now when you develop a view that keeping the lid on the wild mind is something that will magically just result in that mind not being wild anymore, eventually.

244
00:21:18,874 --> 00:21:21,116
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, so, uh, just to...

245
00:21:23,734 --> 00:21:27,916
[SPEAKER_01]: One suffers, and then you get... you just... you choose the path of punishing yourself.

246
00:21:28,556 --> 00:21:35,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if I feel that I punish myself, then things will be okay, in some sense.

247
00:21:35,099 --> 00:21:35,960
[SPEAKER_01]: I just...

248
00:21:35,980 --> 00:21:41,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a classic example would be, like, I break the precepts.

249
00:21:42,062 --> 00:21:42,863
[SPEAKER_01]: I try and keep the precepts.

250
00:21:43,223 --> 00:21:44,443
[SPEAKER_01]: I break the precepts, whatever.

251
00:21:45,324 --> 00:21:45,864
[SPEAKER_01]: I...

252
00:21:47,660 --> 00:21:51,202
[SPEAKER_01]: I lied to someone, or whatever, sexual activity, whatever.

253
00:21:52,223 --> 00:21:53,544
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel guilty on that.

254
00:21:53,784 --> 00:22:00,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you then determine, well, that guilt is, you know, suffering, and I shouldn't do that, and that was wrong.

255
00:22:01,249 --> 00:22:03,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I will not eat for... Next ten days.

256
00:22:03,650 --> 00:22:05,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, next ten days, I'm not going to eat.

257
00:22:05,371 --> 00:22:06,192
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not going to look at anybody.

258
00:22:06,212 --> 00:22:06,912
[SPEAKER_00]: To purify myself.

259
00:22:06,972 --> 00:22:07,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

260
00:22:07,813 --> 00:22:10,634
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're trying to make this... That's apples and oranges, basically.

261
00:22:10,815 --> 00:22:12,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, nothing to do with one another.

262
00:22:12,596 --> 00:22:13,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

263
00:22:13,418 --> 00:22:19,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just to say, it's kind of trying to make a deal, like you determining what the price is.

264
00:22:19,541 --> 00:22:21,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

265
00:22:21,582 --> 00:22:23,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And then making a deal with yourself.

266
00:22:23,123 --> 00:22:23,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

267
00:22:23,543 --> 00:22:24,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, that's a good deal.

268
00:22:24,543 --> 00:22:25,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, thank you.

269
00:22:26,264 --> 00:22:27,325
[SPEAKER_01]: I can do that, you know.

270
00:22:28,085 --> 00:22:36,309
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you start doing that, just the more pain you feel for not eating, you think that this is paying for what I've done.

271
00:22:36,369 --> 00:22:37,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'm paying, yeah.

272
00:22:37,670 --> 00:22:39,691
[SPEAKER_00]: But who are you to... Who are you to decide the price, yeah.

273
00:22:39,931 --> 00:22:40,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

274
00:22:40,591 --> 00:22:42,632
[SPEAKER_01]: You're in no position, because you're the one that owes the money.

275
00:22:44,131 --> 00:22:50,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is, you break the precepts, you fail, you acknowledge that to that extent, you confess it.

276
00:22:50,993 --> 00:22:53,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Guilt comes up, you don't try to get rid of the guilt.

277
00:22:53,975 --> 00:22:56,375
[SPEAKER_00]: If it comes up on account of... like rational guilt.

278
00:22:57,916 --> 00:23:02,678
[SPEAKER_00]: If it comes up on account of you factually breaking the precepts, well, let it sink in.

279
00:23:04,878 --> 00:23:09,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, if the mind starts revolving around it irrationally, well, you recognize that.

280
00:23:10,140 --> 00:23:11,401
[SPEAKER_00]: You still don't try to get rid of it.

281
00:23:12,002 --> 00:23:15,623
[SPEAKER_00]: You just restrain your mental input towards the irrational guilt.

282
00:23:16,603 --> 00:23:18,324
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't try to get rid of the state of mind.

283
00:23:22,105 --> 00:23:23,185
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you learn from it.

284
00:23:23,745 --> 00:23:36,149
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how the Buddha would say, like, yes, transgressing and then confessing it, recognizing your fault to that extent, is how you grow in this training.

285
00:23:38,170 --> 00:23:39,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Not beating yourself up.

286
00:23:39,711 --> 00:23:44,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, or like not confessing, and then just doing some random things as a penance.

287
00:23:46,033 --> 00:23:49,553
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, you know where the penance is.

288
00:23:50,954 --> 00:23:52,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Bringing it out to the light.

289
00:23:53,594 --> 00:23:54,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Acknowledging it.

290
00:23:55,155 --> 00:24:07,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and it's like, if I act out of lust, well, the only way that I'm going to really pay for it, so to speak, and overcome the consequences of all my lustful actions,

291
00:24:09,870 --> 00:24:12,512
[SPEAKER_01]: To me, the only thing that you would do is to uproot the lust.

292
00:24:12,532 --> 00:24:13,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Uproot the lust, of course.

293
00:24:13,552 --> 00:24:13,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Forever.

294
00:24:13,892 --> 00:24:14,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So you never do it again.

295
00:24:14,953 --> 00:24:15,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

296
00:24:15,353 --> 00:24:16,594
[SPEAKER_01]: That would mean that you are...

297
00:24:16,614 --> 00:24:18,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Truly rehabilitated.

298
00:24:18,075 --> 00:24:18,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Rehabilitated, yeah.

299
00:24:18,876 --> 00:24:19,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

300
00:24:19,676 --> 00:24:22,057
[SPEAKER_01]: You are... That's like the confession, you know.

301
00:24:22,378 --> 00:24:25,600
[SPEAKER_01]: I acted out of lust because I...

302
00:24:25,620 --> 00:24:27,040
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to act out of lust.

303
00:24:27,181 --> 00:24:27,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

304
00:24:27,581 --> 00:24:30,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And I still maintain that lust.

305
00:24:31,023 --> 00:24:34,225
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I'm really sorry, then I should give up lust forever.

306
00:24:34,265 --> 00:24:37,207
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the actual purity comes from.

307
00:24:37,867 --> 00:24:38,708
[SPEAKER_00]: The purity of the mind.

308
00:24:39,567 --> 00:24:46,969
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're actually uprooting the lust, not just adhering to the traditional observances and so on.

309
00:24:49,170 --> 00:24:51,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, you know, I never break the precepts, I'm pure.

310
00:24:51,210 --> 00:24:56,172
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, you never break the precepts means you're not making things worse, but you're far from pure.

311
00:25:00,373 --> 00:25:06,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, precepts prevent you from adding, as we said before, adding fuel to that fire, but they do not

312
00:25:07,613 --> 00:25:09,554
[SPEAKER_00]: They do not uproot the problem in and of themselves.

313
00:25:09,654 --> 00:25:14,137
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to make the effort and tame that animal.

314
00:25:14,177 --> 00:25:34,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, fully uproot and extinguish those desires of that untrained wild mind and all those tendencies that it has, by containing it, by staying with it, by seeing through it, by presenting it with right perceptions at the right time, all the other, like, higher modes of training that we have talked about.

315
00:25:36,161 --> 00:25:37,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning anicca...

316
00:25:37,562 --> 00:25:48,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning anicca, unattractiveness, loving-kindness, all those things that you would be bringing to the mind, depending on its state, in order for you to communicate to it, to teach it.

